@startuml
title POST /api/ai-agents-teams/{address}/{id}/unpublish — stop a published team answering mentions
skinparam backgroundColor #F8FBFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #294C60
skinparam sequence {
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #EAF5FF
  ParticipantBorderColor #38789E
  LifeLineBorderColor #6A92AA
  GroupBackgroundColor #FFF3E8
  GroupBorderColor #D97638
}

actor "Caller" as Caller #FFD8C2
participant "Client\n(generated AIAgentsTeamsApi)" as Client #D5EAF7
participant "Embers API" as API #FFC9A8
participant "AgentsTeamsService" as Domain #CDEACC
participant "F1r3sky\n(PDS)" as Firesky #FFDFC4

Caller -> Client : agentsTeamsUnpublish
Client -> API : POST .../{address}/{id}/unpublish\n(no body, no signature)
API -> Domain : unpublish(address, id)
Domain -> Domain : resolve the published deployment;\nload the SERVICE-HELD credential\nfor its URI
alt team is published and credentials resolve
  Domain -> Firesky : log in with the stored\nper-publication app password
  Domain -> Firesky : delete the\ncom.f1r3sky.agentsteam.config record\n(repo addressed by did, falling back\nto handle)
  Firesky --> Domain : record removed
  Domain --> API : ok
  API --> Client : 200, no body
  Client --> Caller : the handle stops answering mentions
else not published, credentials missing,\nor a legacy publication without did/handle
  API <-- Domain : error
  Client <-- API : 500 InternalError
end

note over API #FFF6C9
This mutation sits OUTSIDE the two-phase signing
protocol: the HTTP call is unauthenticated, and
the PDS mutation is performed with credentials
only the Embers service holds. The account and
handle are kept — republishing reverses it.
end note

note over Domain #FFF0C7
The on-chain credential stack exposes only push
and toList: a publication is permanent history.
What varies is the config record's presence,
which is exactly what published-state reports
as "active".
end note
@enduml
